.Dd October 17, 2026
.Dt ADJKERNTZ 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm adjkerntz
.Nd tell the kernel the local time zone's offset from UTC
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl a
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The system clock keeps UTC.
A few on-disk formats store local time instead, the FAT file system's timestamps
among them, so the kernel needs the local zone's offset to convert:
.Nm
sets it.
.Pp
The option is as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl a
Read the offset in effect now from
.Pa /etc/localtime
and set the
.Va kern.utc_offset
.Xr sysctl 8
variable, in minutes east of UTC, if it differs.
Without
.Pa /etc/localtime
the offset is 0.
The
.Ev TZ
environment variable is ignored: the kernel's offset is the system's, not a
user's.
.El
.Pp
.Xr rc 8
runs
.Nm
.Fl a
at boot, before any file system is checked or mounted.
The system
.Xr crontab 5
runs it again at one minute past every hour, so the offset follows daylight
saving time, and
.Xr tzsetup 8
runs it after changing the zone.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/localtime -compact
.It Pa /etc/localtime
The local time zone.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr tzfile 5 ,
.Xr mount_msdosfs 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8 ,
.Xr tzsetup 8
.Sh HISTORY
An
.Nm
command appeared in
.Fx 1.0 ,
where it also adjusts a hardware clock kept in local time.
The
.Va kern.utc_offset
variable is
.Ox Ns 's
name; there it is the hardware clock's offset.
.Sh BUGS
Times are converted with the offset in effect now, not the one in effect at the
time converted: a file written in summer shows an hour off in winter, as it does
on
.Fx
and Windows.
